\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The same $500$ held-out problems, solved five other ways. Classical timings are one CPU core and one query; the learned rows are one GPU and $20$ samples per query, so the time column is not a like-for-like comparison. Classical clearance is averaged over solved problems; the learned rows are per-sample rates at $250$ training environments}}{7}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:baselines}{{1}{7}{The same $500$ held-out problems, solved five other ways. Classical timings are one CPU core and one query; the learned rows are one GPU and $20$ samples per query, so the time column is not a like-for-like comparison. Classical clearance is averaged over solved problems; the learned rows are per-sample rates at $250$ training environments}{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Where the learned planner actually sits. The world-frame arm lies on the straight-line floor; the reduction lifts it by $30.2$ points; the classical planners remain far above. We show this rather than omit it: the contribution here is the representation result, not a competitive planner}}{7}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:baselines}{{1}{7}{Where the learned planner actually sits. The world-frame arm lies on the straight-line floor; the reduction lifts it by $30.2$ points; the classical planners remain far above. We show this rather than omit it: the contribution here is the representation result, not a competitive planner}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The untrained stochastic baseline: Brownian bridges pinned at the endpoints, $20$ samples per query, same collision check. Widening the prior buys nothing at any setting, so best-of-$N$ over random detours is not what a learned sampler is competing against}}{8}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:prior}{{2}{8}{The untrained stochastic baseline: Brownian bridges pinned at the endpoints, $20$ samples per query, same collision check. Widening the prior buys nothing at any setting, so best-of-$N$ over random detours is not what a learned sampler is competing against}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {C}The reduction scales; the baseline does not}{8}{subsection.5.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Held-out performance against training-set size. ``+aug.'' is the world-frame arm trained with random $\mathrm  {SE}(3)$ motions of the whole problem. The world-frame arm gains $2.6$ points across a $12.5\times $ increase in environments and augmentation adds at most $2.1$ more, while the reduction gains $26.6$ and has not plateaued. The $60$-environment row is a mean over three seeds; the rest are single seed. The last column is the deployment metric, best-of-$20$ behind a collision check}}{8}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:main}{{3}{8}{Held-out performance against training-set size. ``+aug.'' is the world-frame arm trained with random $\SE $ motions of the whole problem. The world-frame arm gains $2.6$ points across a $12.5\times $ increase in environments and augmentation adds at most $2.1$ more, while the reduction gains $26.6$ and has not plateaued. The $60$-environment row is a mean over three seeds; the rest are single seed. The last column is the deployment metric, best-of-$20$ behind a collision check}{table.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The gap widens with data. If canonicalisation were compensating for a shortage of training data one would expect it to close; instead the world-frame baseline is nearly flat while the reduced model continues to improve. The hollow marker is the no-roll ablation, drawn on the treatment line because it is the same arm with one component removed}}{8}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:scaling}{{2}{8}{The gap widens with data. If canonicalisation were compensating for a shortage of training data one would expect it to close; instead the world-frame baseline is nearly flat while the reduced model continues to improve. The hollow marker is the no-roll ablation, drawn on the treatment line because it is the same arm with one component removed}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Decomposition by mechanism on the same held-out set. Among the mechanisms tested, canonicalisation accounts for the effect, and both parts of it matter: recentring and axis alignment contribute $41\%$ and $59\%$ of the total. Neither mechanism built for the sixth degree of freedom is distinguishable from zero. The $\Delta $ column is against the arm without that mechanism, so the rows are not additive; the two indented rows decompose the row above them}}{9}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:ablation}{{4}{9}{Decomposition by mechanism on the same held-out set. Among the mechanisms tested, canonicalisation accounts for the effect, and both parts of it matter: recentring and axis alignment contribute $41\%$ and $59\%$ of the total. Neither mechanism built for the sixth degree of freedom is distinguishable from zero. The $\Delta $ column is against the arm without that mechanism, so the rows are not additive; the two indented rows decompose the row above them}{table.4}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {D}Frame averaging is inert; the roll ablation is unresolved}{9}{subsection.5.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The residual does not predict what a mechanism is worth. Augmentation and canonicalisation leave the learned field equally equivariant, and differ by $28$ points of held-out performance}}{9}{table.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:twosigns}{{5}{9}{The residual does not predict what a mechanism is worth. Augmentation and canonicalisation leave the learned field equally equivariant, and differ by $28$ points of held-out performance}{table.5}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {E}What the residual does not predict}{9}{subsection.5.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:twosigns}{{V-E}{9}{What the residual does not predict}{subsection.5.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces (a) Canonicalisation is seven times the next largest mechanism. The band is $\pm 2$ across-seed standard errors, measured on the treatment arm at $60$ environments; frame averaging sits inside it, and the roll-augmentation bar rests on a single-seed ablation. (b) The non-equivariance residual of Eq.\nobreakspace  {}\eqref  {eq:resid} is flat across a $12.5\times $ range of training data --- it is not something the model acquires from seeing more layouts. The hollow marker shows that removing roll augmentation raises the residual by $64\%$ but leaves it under $3\%$}}{10}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mechanisms}{{3}{10}{(a) Canonicalisation is seven times the next largest mechanism. The band is $\pm 2$ across-seed standard errors, measured on the treatment arm at $60$ environments; frame averaging sits inside it, and the roll-augmentation bar rests on a single-seed ablation. (b) The non-equivariance residual of Eq.~\eqref {eq:resid} is flat across a $12.5\times $ range of training data --- it is not something the model acquires from seeing more layouts. The hollow marker shows that removing roll augmentation raises the residual by $64\%$ but leaves it under $3\%$}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {F}How much of the level is training budget}{10}{subsection.5.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:budget}{{V-F}{10}{How much of the level is training budget}{subsection.5.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The same pattern in a second state space. Per-sample collision-free rates at $250$ environments and the longer training budget, each against the trivial baseline for its own domain --- geodesic interpolation for the rigid body, a straight line for the point mass. In both domains the world-frame arm fails to clear its own floor and the reduction clears it; the ratio between arms agrees to two figures; and the epoch-gain interaction of Sec.\nobreakspace  {}\ref {sec:budget} reproduces in sign and shape. Single seed per cell}}{11}{table.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:se3}{{6}{11}{The same pattern in a second state space. Per-sample collision-free rates at $250$ environments and the longer training budget, each against the trivial baseline for its own domain --- geodesic interpolation for the rigid body, a straight line for the point mass. In both domains the world-frame arm fails to clear its own floor and the reduction clears it; the ratio between arms agrees to two figures; and the epoch-gain interaction of Sec.~\ref {sec:budget} reproduces in sign and shape. Single seed per cell}{table.6}{}}
